Well, first of all, spotlight by default only shows results from the
home directory. You can access spotlight from the command line by
using 'mdfind'. This is less limited than the GUI version. Look at the
man page for more details.

Second, the Finder only shows files that Apple has decided is relevant
to the average user. OS X hides files a few ways; one is to put a dot
"." in front of a file a la *nixes, and another is by setting the file
attributes to invisible. To get around the "." invisbility, you use
"ls -a" to show all files (create a file called .bash_profile in your
home directory (if you use 10.4) and add the line
alias ls='ls -Ahl'
to make life easier for you.)

To show hidden files in the finder you have to edit the relevant plist
file, or use this:
defaults write com.apple.finder AppleShowAllFiles -bool true

This clutters the finder a bit, so I prefer to use the SetFile command
(found in /Developer/Tools/ if you have the Developer Tools installed)
to set the file attriute to visible (try setfile =? for details) for
those directories that I want to access from the finder.

By the way, if you'd like to experience full ANSI nethack, the OS X
Terminal.app isn't going to work for you. As was pointed out to me by
someone else in this newsgroup, OS X is real Unix, so you can use Unix
apps to play Nethack. rxvt is a great lightweight terminal application
that supports ANSI/VGA fonts (and thus use of the IBMGRAPHICS option
in Nethack). You can install this via Darwinports/Macports (just
google it. It's a dead simple ports system). I use vga11x19.pcf as my
Nethack font.

Finally, if you're interested at all in compiling Nethack from source,
I've written a patch that edits the relevant makefiles and such to
successfully compile on an OS X system. There are a few idiosyncrasies
that make compiling Nethack not so simple for OS X users, but if you
check the newsgroup in a couple of days, I'll post a guide and patch
to make it easier.

Oh and for the record: the "no permission to lock perm!" error is due
to the fact that Nethack defaults to setting the group (or maybe it's
the owner) for Nethack to "games" which does not exist by default in
OS X. Simply setting the owner:group to unknown:staff or unknown:admin
by default would solve this problem.

good luck with OS X's subsystem; you should realize that Darwin is not
so different from other *nixes, so the myriad tutorials on Linux and
Unix will be relevant to you also.

